## **COOPI is looking for a Country Logistics Coordinator in Sudan**

**COOPI has been operating in Sudan since 2004**, providing humanitarian assistance and strengthening the resilience of populations affected by conflict, displacement, and climate-related shocks. Through an integrated, multisectoral approach, COOPI implements interventions in Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH), Food Security and Livelihoods (FSL), Shelter and Non-Food Items (NFI), Protection, and Cash-Based Assistance. Its programmes target the most vulnerable populations, including internally displaced persons (IDPs), returnees, refugees, and host communities, with particular attention to women, children, female-headed households, older persons, and persons with disabilities.

Following the outbreak of the conflict on 15 April 2023, COOPI significantly strengthened its emergency response to address the rapidly growing humanitarian needs. With its country coordination recently moved from Port Sudan to Khartoum, COOPI currently operates in North Darfur, and Khartoum, providing life-saving assistance, improving access to essential services, supporting livelihood recovery, and strengthening community-based protection mechanisms.

**Across its interventions, COOPI mainstreams Protection, Gender, Disability Inclusion, Disaster Risk Reduction (DRR), Climate Adaptation, and Accountability to Affected Populations (AAP). Its approach combines immediate humanitarian assistance with early recovery and resilience-building initiatives, promoting community participation, local partnerships, and sustainable solutions.**

COOPI implements its humanitarian programmes in collaboration with local and international partners, with support from **institutional donors, including the Italian Agency for Development Cooperation (AICS), the European Union, and United Nations agencies.**

## **Description of the role**

At the mission level, **the Country Logistics Coordinator, together with his dedicated team, is responsible for all logistics and security activities in the country** and plays a supporting, advisory and monitoring role vis-à-vis the project leaders and logistics assistants in the provinces to ensure that COOPI procedures and rules on procurement, logistics, document storage, equipment management and asset storage, and risk management (security, health, fraud) are known, understood and adhered to. **Capacity building is an essential aspect of the position.**

## **Responsibilities**

**Ensuring the logistical coordination of the country**

- **Carry out, follow-up or validate (as appropriate) the procurement procedures, supply of goods/services, transport systems and technical organisation of the work of all COOPI field offices in the country, as defined by the procedures;**
- **Ensure the practical and administrative management of the warehouses and inventory** of COOPI-owned and project-purchased durable goods;
- Coordinate the country logistics function at the national coordination level and provide **monitoring/support to the operational bases;**
- **Ensure the planning and organisation** of all logistical activities at national level;
- Ensure the proper functioning of logistics in terms of quality and quantity, at all different levels and in compliance with procedures;
- Ensure that **price/supplier databases** are up-to-date and regularly compared with those of similar organisations;
- Provide up-to-date costs and an up-to-date situation of the vehicle and equipment fleet for the preparation of new budgets, at the request of the Country Coordinator;

**Ensuring the application and implementation of procurement and procedures**

- **Ensure at national level the correct application of procedures - COOPI and donors** - concerning the procurement of goods and services (including planning and necessary documentation);
- **Monitor supplier performance** in accordance with COOPI standards;
- Properly monitor the delivery and archiving of all documentation produced in the process of acquiring goods/services, both nationally and internationally;
- **Verify and participate, if necessary, in the selection process of suppliers of goods and services and in the awarding of contracts for tenders and framework contracts**, with the aim of transparency and consistency;
- Provide adequate support concerning the elaboration of the **project procurement plan;**
- Use the **COOPI software** to monitor the management and archiving of document scans that constitute the procedures in the software.

**Coordinating and managing the vehicle fleet**

- For coordination, ensure the **administrative and operational management of the vehicle fleet, machines and electric generators;**
- In the field, monitor and control the correct administrative and operational management of the vehicle fleet, machines and electric generators, both through **monthly logistical reports and field visits;**
- Based on monthly logistical reports or through field inspections, check that the maintenance and repair of vehicles are carried out correctly and that monitoring reports and periodic checks are carried out correctly;
- **Ensure compliance** with the organisation's procedures and country regulations with regard to the use of vehicles and machines.

**Asset management**

- It is responsible for **monitoring durable goods** in coordination through the use of the tools provided by the COOPI Logistics Manual;
- Ensure the monitoring of durable goods on the ground using the tools provided by the COOPI logistics manual;
- Ensure that durable goods are properly maintained and that there are rules for the proper use of goods in the country;
- Carry out regular updates of the coordination and field project inventories: verify through the analysis of equipment monitoring or during field visits.

**Stock management**

- Carry out - and ensure based on - the **monitoring of storage warehouses and the use of reporting tools** by analysing stock monitoring in the monthly logistics report or during field visits;
- **Ensure that all goods are included in the inventory** and checked on entry and exit from the storage space according to COOPI procedures, as well as their identification and allocation to COOPI or a specific project.

**Computer systems management and maintenance**

- In coordination, ensure the management of the installation, maintenance and surveillance of **IT, communications, radio (HF, VHF) and satellite equipment;**
- **Provide adequate training** and support to personnel regarding the procedures for using the above-mentioned equipment.

**Security**

- It is responsible for **managing the security of the organisation's assets and property;**
- **Collaborate and assist the Country Coordinator / Project Managers** on security aspects of expatriate and local staff;
- Develop and implement a specific **security plan and Standard Operating Procedures (SoP)** for the bases of competence;
- Provide assistance and support for the development of security plans and Standard Operating Procedures (SoP) of the bases;
- Contribute to the collection of security information and implement preventive actions and mechanisms;
- **Ensure the dissemination of security procedures** by scheduling briefings with new staff and regular updates with all staff;
- In coordination and at the bases, in cooperation with personnel on the ground, ensure adequate security conditions for the organisation's assets and property.

**Ensuring personnel management**

- **Coordinate, supervise and evaluate personnel** in hierarchical relation;
- **Organise the training of logistics staff** working on country projects (identification of team weaknesses and targeted training);
- Ensuring compliance with residency and/or entry authorisation procedures in the country;
- Ensuring COOPI staff are welcomed at the airport and arranging accommodation for expatriate staff.

**Ensuring the reporting and documentation of logistics**

- Ensuring that all reports related to the logistics sector are produced as stipulated by COOPI procedures and the Country Rules (vehicle and goods management, supplies, purchasing, warehousing, personnel reports, etc.).

**Institutional relations**

- On behalf of the Country Coordinator, represent the organisation during logistics cluster meetings and security briefings (INSO, UNDSS, Embassies, etc.).

## **Requirements**

**ESSENTIAL**

- **Degree in Logistics or higher education or LLA (Logistics Learning Alliance) certification in Humanitarian Logistics;**
- **Working experience in humanitarian logistics of at least 5 years;**
- **Previous experience in logistics coordination of at least 1 to 2 years** in similar organizations (in terms of size and constraints);
- Indispensable knowledge of the **English language**;
- Good command of common software;
- Strong organizational skills;
- Willingness to motivate and accompany teams in emergency contexts;
- **Good physical condition and ability to travel and stay in remote or unsafe areas;**
- Excellent negotiation and communication skills;
- Result orientation, drive for improvement, creativity, initiative;
- **Interest in training, supporting and consulting multicultural teams;**
- Ability to work under pressure and autonomously.

**DESIRABLE**

- Experience with COOPI and knowledge of its logistics procedures.;
- **Previous experience in Sudan.**

We reserve the right to close the vacancy prior to the application closing date, once a sufficient number of applications has been received. **Please note that only the shortlisted candidates will be contacted.**

*COOPI Cooperazione Internazionale is an Italian secular and independent humanitarian organisation fighting against all forms of poverty to improve the world. Founded in 1965, today COOPI is present in 27 countries in Africa, Latin America and the Caribbean and the Middle East, with both emergency and development projects carried out by expatriate staff – an average of over 200 people per year – and national staff. The main institutional funders are the European Union – ECHO and INTPA – USAID, UN agencies, the Italian Government, local authorities and other European Governments.*

The International Rescue Committee (IRC) responds to the world's worst humanitarian crises, helping to restore health, safety, education, economic wellbeing, and power to people devastated by conflict and disaster. Founded in 1933 at the call of Albert Einstein, the IRC is one of the world's largest international humanitarian non-governmental organizations (INGO), at work in more than 40 countries and 29 U.S. cities helping people to survive, reclaim control of their future and strengthen their communities. A force for humanity, IRC employees deliver lasting impact by restoring safety, dignity and hope to millions. If you're a solutions-driven, passionate change-maker, come join us in positively impacting the lives of millions of people world-wide for a better future.

**Job Background / Overview:**

The External Relations (ER) department includes private fundraising, marketing & communications and advocacy. ER’s goal is to mobilize external audiences to raise funding to meet core organization needs deliver programs that support our clients to recover and gain control of their lives; influence policy changes to improve outcomes for our clients; and build and grow our global brand.

The Officer, Prospect Research & Management, sits within External Relations’ Prospect Research & Management team, supporting the IRC’s sustainability by finding opportunities for funding, focusing our resources, and protecting our reputation through due diligence. This is a 12-month fixed-term position. They are responsible for rigorously synthesizing data and information sources, providing senior staff and frontline fundraisers with high-quality research and actionable insights on US-based donors, as well as strategic feedback for board meetings, donor meetings, and events. They manage portfolio relationships with Officer-level fundraisers, delivering to agreed strategies and influencing decision-making through research-derived insights. The right candidate will be comfortable producing core prospect research products, and will build positive relationships across internal teams and external vendors, while delivering superior research and reporting services that ultimately increase philanthropic support for our clients.

**Major Responsibilities:**

- Deliver high-quality confidential biographical, financial, and due diligence research profiles on US-based HNWI donors and prospects within agreed deadlines and parameters, drawing on a range of online and electronic sources;
- Build robust pipelines for fundraisers focused on US market by identifying, rating, and qualifying HNWI donors capable of significant financial support;
- Manage portfolio relationships with Officer-level frontline fundraising colleagues in the US, delivering to agreed strategies and developing business-oriented recommendations for donors and prospects, with the broader goal to increase support for the IRC’s clients;
- Translate research into actionable insights, integrating data-driven and qualitative analyses and broader trends into tactics and strategies for prospect/donor management, opportunity development, and maximized revenue;
- Contribute ideas to improve tools and methods that support comprehensive prospect research and portfolio management, including relationship mapping, analytics, and other appropriate metrics;
- Manage a suite of resources (e.g. CRM, subscription databases, media tools) to understand and respond to specific opportunities;
- Contribute to special projects (e.g. vendor screenings, data mining, ad hoc reports, etc.) and conduct proactive research projects in partnership with colleagues across the organization, identifying new opportunities that align with stakeholder teams’ strategies;
- Collaborate with prospect research-adjacent teams (Philanthropy account managers, Program Services team) to ensure a smooth and efficient overall service to IRC’s high value fundraising teams, actively seeking and incorporating stakeholder feedback;
- Follow team best practice and process guidance, and produce documentation to support team protocols;
- Work with line manager to prioritize a varied workload (research profiles, reports, ad hoc analysis), adapting to changing stakeholder priorities while meeting established timelines

**Essential Qualifications:**

- Bachelor’s degree required; Master’s degree preferred
- 3–5 years’ experience applying quantitative and qualitative analysis and business intelligence in support of non-profit fundraising, with familiarity with US markets;
- High proficiency in internet search engines and sophisticated search techniques, fundraising software, data retrieval services (e.g. LexisNexis, iWave, Foundation Directory, etc.), MS Office (Word, Outlook and Excel, in particular).
- Experience working with a relational database is essential, preferably Salesforce

**Demonstrated Skills and Competencies:**

- Research/database management (Salesforce);
- Analyzing, interpreting, and synthesizing data, and translating findings into actionable recommendations;
- Ability to apply and contribute to proactive prospecting methodologies that support organizational goals, including data mining and sophisticated constituent database queries;
- Excellent verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to explain complex research clearly, concisely, and in an actionable way

**Desired Qualifications:**

- Keen understanding of philanthropic trends, in particular the impacts on humanitarian aid sector
- Knowledge and curiosity about emerging concepts and practices in the field of strategic philanthropy, corporate social responsibility, Public Private Partnerships, and impact investing, particularly as it relates to U.S. entities;
- Strong interpersonal and time-management skills; confirmed ability to prioritize and deliver on time;
- Ability to act with sensitivity and discretion while working with highly confidential information;
- Prior experience working at an international organization outside the US.

**Working Environment:**

This role may require working remotely full- or part-time (i.e., telecommuting). Applicants must have a home or alternate workspace they can effectively complete their work from during regularly scheduled hours.

At the IRC, we seek to build a welcoming, inclusive environment not only for our refugee clients, but for all of our employees. As an equal opportunity employer, we value the strength found in the diversity of voices, experiences, and backgrounds who join us in our work. Fostering an inclusive culture with equitable practices is everyone's responsibility, and we regularly offer trainings and opportunities to learn more and find ways to do better. We do not discriminate on the basis of race, religion, color, national origin, sex, gender, gender expression, sexual orientation, age, marital status, veteran status, socioeconomic status, or disability status. We are dedicated to ensuring that disabled individuals are provided reasonable accommodation to participate in the job application or interview process, to perform essential job functions, and to receive other benefits and privileges of employment. Please contact us to request accommodation. In your application, please feel free to indicate your preferred pronouns.

We recognize that few applicants may "check all the boxes," but that each person has their own unique strengths that they would bring to the table. In our commitment to building a diverse and authentic work culture, we invite you to apply anyway. You belong here.

**Compensation:** ($72,040 - $81,000/annually). Ranges are based on various factors including the labor market, job type, internal equity, and budget. Exact offers are calibrated by work location, individual candidate experience and skills relative to the defined job requirements.

**Professional Standards:** All International Rescue Committee workers must adhere to the core values and principles outlined in [IRC Way - Standards for Professional Conduct](https://www.rescue.org/page/our-code-conduct). Our Standards are Integrity, Service, Equality and Accountability. In accordance with these values, the IRC operates and enforces policies on [Safeguarding](https://www.rescue.org/page/safeguarding), Conflicts of Interest, Fiscal Integrity, and Reporting Wrongdoing and Protection from Retaliation. IRC is committed to take all necessary preventive measures and create an environment where people feel safe, and to take all necessary actions and corrective measures when harm occurs. IRC builds teams of professionals who promote critical reflection, power sharing, debate, and objectivity to deliver the best possible services to our clients.

**Benefits:** IRC offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package. Highlights include 10 paid sick leave, 10 U.S. holidays, 20-25 generous paid time off based on role and tenure, medical insurance starting at $163/month, dental starting at $6.50/month, and vision starting at $5/month, flexible spending accounts (healthcare, dependent care, and commuter), a 403(b) retirement savings plan with employer match (immediate vesting) along with a non-elective employer contribution based on age and years of service (3-year vesting), disability and life insurance, and an Employee Assistance Program (EAP) offering confidential counseling and support services for employees and their families.

**Equal Opportunity Employer:** IRC is an Equal Opportunity Employer. IRC considers all applicants on the basis of merit without regard to race, sex, color, national origin, religion, sexual orientation, age, marital status, veteran status, disability or any other characteristic protected by applicable law.

At DCAF, we envision a world where democratic sector governance advances sustainable peace and development and human dignity for all.

Our mission is to advance the security of all people through nationally owned, inclusive, and participatory security sector reforms based on international norms and good practices.

DCAF contributes to making peace and development more sustainable by assisting partner states and international actors supporting these states, to improve the governance of their security sector through inclusive and participatory reforms. It creates innovative knowledge products, promotes norms and good practices, provides legal and policy advice and supports capacity‐building of both state and non‐state security sector stakeholders.

For our Africa-Americas Entity, we are seeking a dedicated and dynamic individual to be our next **Head of Country Office, The Gambia (Africa Americas)**.

**Location: Banjul, The Gambia**

**Starting date: January 4, 2027**

**Work rate: 100%**

**Duration: 9 months, with possibility of extension upon availability of funding**

**The role**

The Head of Country Office (HoCO) will provide overall leadership to DCAF’s programme and engagement in support to the SSR process in The Gambia. The current programme, launched in January 2026, aims to support sustainable institutional change within the Gambia Immigration Department and improve service delivery by and accountability of security personnel to the communities, especially at the borders. This work builds on DCAF’s support to SSR in The Gambia since 2018.

The HoCO will be based in Banjul and will represent DCAF in The Gambia. They will provide both strategic and technical support and will manage and mentor DCAF’s team in Banjul. Hierarchically reporting to the Head/Deputy Head of Entity, they work in close collaboration with the Geneva based Senior Manager.

Key areas of operation

**\*Programme management**

- Manage all aspects of the day-to-day implementation of DCAF’s programme and oversee a portfolio of program components with diverse partners;
- In close coordination with the Senior Manager, develop and review the work plan and budget monitoring of the country programme on a monthly basis and take the necessary corrective measures;
- Supervise, monitor and evaluate project implementation including data collection, internal or independent evaluations, course correction or iteration;
- Oversee the development of terms of reference for activities and associated documents, as well as the drafting of reports of activities;
- Oversee execution of budget approved by management;
- Monitor, analyse and provide written reports of political developments and program results in The Gambia;
- Oversee the development of DCAF Banjul monthly reports providing updates of political and security context, programme implementation, national and international partners relations and engagements;
- Actively engage with international and national partners to be abreast of developments in the sector and potential new opportunities to support SSGR;
- Regularly update the project risk matrix through the conduct of ongoing political economy analysis.

**\*Office Management**

- Manage and mentor DCAF’s team in Banjul;
- Ensure the management of human resources within the office including the distribution of tasks and workload, recruitment, contracting, training, integration of staff members and national consultants, as well as periodic evaluations;
- Ensure the proper application of financial and administrative procedures and supervise the action of the resource manager and project officers;
- Maintain contacts with the Gambian government and ensure the compliance of the office's activities with local legislation and compliance with fiscal, social and health obligations.
- Ensure the security of the office, staff members, and DCAF equipment.

**\*Representation**

- Represent DCAF with national authorities and partners; provide expertise to national partners and at public events as requested;
- Maintain contacts and coordinate DCAF's action with security sector stakeholders and DCAF partners in The Gambia, including the Executive, Legislative, defence and security institutions, civil society, community leaders, think tanks and the media, for the purpose of the implementation of the current programme and opportunities and support new initiatives;
- Ensure close coordination and communication with international, multilateral and non-governmental organizations and embassies;
- In coordination with the Senior Manager based in Geneva, identify opportunities with diplomatic missions in country to spotlight the DCAF programme and explore potential new sources of funding.
- Research, communication and training
- Develop and plan tailor-made capacity building activities for defence and security institutions and external SSR oversight actors, in line with the changing local and regional context;
- Support and contribute to research initiatives within the framework of projects and actively participate in the dissemination of results;
- Prepare the content of thematic communications on SSGR in collaboration with associated DCAF experts and facilitate exchanges in project training-workshops;
- Support communications initiatives aimed at developing DCAF's visibility and reputation (including on social media).

**Your experience**

- Advanced university degree in security studies, political science, law, international relations or a related field; a combination of relevant academic qualifications and extensive experience may be accepted in lieu of the advanced university degree
- At least 10 years’ experience working with multilateral stakeholders in fragile contexts, with proven technical expertise in the area of security and justice sector governance and reform preferably in Sub-Saharan Africa
- Demonstrated ability to conduct political analysis, and experience in working at the political level.
- Proven experience in managing programmes, including project planning and design, budget management, project implementation and monitoring and evaluation
- Experience in managing teams and staff in complex environments.
- Excellent communications skills, and a perfect command of written and spoken English
- Excellent organisation skills with strong attention to detail
- Familiarity with the UN, EU, AU, ECOWAS, and other intergovernmental and/or regional organisations

**We offer**

- a rewarding, dynamic and challenging work experience
- the chance to be part of a multicultural team of supportive, hardworking and values-driven people
- the chance to contribute to improving security of states and their people within a framework of democratic governance, the rule of law, and respect for human rights

Tiered Approach

In line with the commitment to safeguard capacity and support personnel already in the Organization, a majority of UNDP UNCDF/UNV vacancies are advertised using a tiered application process whereby:

- Tier 0 : UNDP/UNCDF/UNV IP staff holding permanent (PA) and fixed-term (FTA) appointments, whose posts will be abolished, or contracts will be terminated or not renewed during 2026.

- Tier 1 : Other UNDP/UNCDF/UNV staff holding permanent (PA) and fixed-term (FTA) appointments

- Tier 2 : UNDP/UNCDF/UNV staff holding temporary appointments (TA), personnel on regular PSA contracts, and Expert and Specialist UN Volunteers

- Tier 3 or no tier indicated : All other contract types from UNDP/UNCDF/UNV and other agencies, and other external candidates

Please make note of the Tier(s) indicated in the vacancy title, if any, and ensure that you satisfy the eligibility to apply.

Background

Diversity, Equity and Inclusion are core principles at UNDP: we value diversity as an expression of the multiplicity of nations and cultures where we operate, we foster inclusion as a way of ensuring all personnel are empowered to contribute to our mission, and we ensure equity and fairness in all our actions. Taking a 'leave no one behind' approach to our diversity efforts means increasing representation of underserved populations. People who identify as belonging to marginalized or excluded populations are strongly encouraged to apply. UNDP does not tolerate sexual exploitation and abuse, any kind of harassment, including sexual harassment, and discrimination. All selected candidates will therefore undergo rigorous reference and background checks.

The United Nations Development Program (UNDP) works with governments, development partners, financial institutions, the private sector and communities to advance sustainable development and strengthen resilience. Through its work on sustainable finance, UNDP supports countries to mobilize and align public and private finance with national development priorities and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).

The SDG Finance and Investment Portfolio strengthens Tanzania's financing ecosystem by mobilizing diversified finance to accelerate the SDGs. As a collaborative platform, it convenes government, the private sector, financial institutions, civil society and development partners to address financing gaps and enable investment. Its work spans public finance for the SDGs; investment promotion and facilitation; and insurance and risk finance. Across these areas, the Portfolio develops investment pipelines, tests financing instruments, promotes SDG-aligned investment, supports sustainable debt and public financing, and builds resilience, protects lives and advances inclusive economic growth. The Program Associate will provide portfolio coordination, administration, procurement, stakeholder engagement, monitoring, reporting, communications, event-management and knowledge-management support. Under matrix reporting, the incumbent will report directly to the National Program Coordinator for functional supervision and work under the Head of the Portfolio's strategic guidance and portfolio-level oversight.

Scope of Work

1. Support program and project planning, implementation and coordination:

- Support preparation, implementation and updating workplans, activity schedules, results frameworks, project trackers, and deliverable schedules in consultation with responsible managers;

- Coordinate day-to-day follow-up of assigned projects and workstreams; track milestones, deadlines, risks, deliverables and stakeholder commitments; and flag emerging issues for timely management action;

- Coordinate strategic and technical meetings, high-level forums and physical or virtual project team meetings; manage invitations, agendas, background materials, presentations, minutes, transcription or translation, action trackers and follow-up;

- Support consultations, workshops, training, launches, innovation challenges, investment events and forums, field activities and missions, working with internal units, partners, consultants and service providers;

- Draft or contribute to terms of reference, concept notes, project documents, implementation plans, presentations, correspondence, high-level briefs and meeting packs.

2. Provide administrative, procurement, event-management and operational support:

- Support preparation and monitoring of project budgets, procurement plans, implementation schedules and administrative trackers in accordance with applicable UNDP rules, policies and procedures:

- Support e-requisitions, procurement, contracting, consultant, vendor, travel and payment processes, including non-PO transactions, in accordance with assigned system roles and UNDP procedures;

- Coordinate event logistics and service providers for meetings, high-level forums, investment and innovation events and communication assignments, including venue, graphic-design, photography and videography services;

- Maintain consultant and contract deliverable schedules, vendor and payment-supporting documents, programme correspondence, project files and other complete, organized and auditready records.

3. Support stakeholder, partnership and thematic portfolio engagement:

- Support engagement with government institutions, regulators, United Nations entities, development partners, financial institutions, investors, businesses, innovation actors, civil society, academia, media and other stakeholders;

- Support activities across the Portfolio's thematic areas, including public finance, investment promotion and facilitation, innovation, insurance and risk finance, and related sustainable-finance initiatives;

- Prepare stakeholder briefs, partner profiles, high-level speeches, talking points, presentations, correspondence and meeting materials and maintain engagement calendars, databases and records of commitments and decisions;

- Facilitate information flow among stakeholders, consultants and UNDP teams and support followup on partnerships, joint activities, events, knowledge platforms and resource-mobilization opportunities.

4. Support project monitoring, reporting and knowledge management:

- Collect, consolidate, review and synthesize implementation and results updates from projects, consultants and partners, ensuring completeness, quality, consistency and timely submission;

- Maintain trackers for activities, outputs, indicators, deliverables, risks and agreed actions and support monitoring against approved workplans and results frameworks;

- Contribute to project, progress, donor, corporate and annual reports, management updates, presentations and newsletters that communicate results to internal and external audiences;

- Document progress, challenges, lessons learned, good practices, impact stories, stakeholder insights and recommendations and maintain supporting evidence for reported results and adaptive programming.

5. Support strategic communications, advocacy and content development:

- Contribute to project and portfolio communication strategies, advocacy campaigns and content plans aligned with program objectives, UNDP branding and partner-visibility requirements;

- Draft and edit high-level briefs, speeches, talking points, press releases, media advisories, articles, blogs, human-interest stories, fact sheets, knowledge briefs, newsletters, presentations, infographics and multimedia content;

- Coordinate content publication and dissemination across websites, social media, email, print and events; support media outreach; and ensure editorial quality, consistent messaging and partner visibility;

- Monitor digital analytics, media coverage and public engagement to improve communications and advocacy, and maintain an organized repository of content, communication assets and knowledge products;

- Perform other duties within the functional profile as deemed necessary for the efficient functioning of the Office and the Organization.

The incumbent performs other duties within their functional profile as deemed necessary for the efficient

functioning of the Office and the Organization.

Institutional Arrangements

The Program Associate will be part of UNDP Tanzania's SDG Finance and Investment Portfolio and will operate under a matrix reporting arrangement. The incumbent will report directly to the National Program Coordinator, who will provide day-to-day functional supervision, task assignment, and quality assurance for assigned activities.

The Head of the Portfolio will provide overall strategic guidance and portfolio-level oversight, ensuring that the incumbent's broader contribution remains aligned with the Portfolio's priorities and strategic direction.

The incumbent will support projects across the Portfolio in accordance with approved workplans and priorities, working closely with relevant UNDP teams, program and project managers, government counterparts, consultants, implementing partners, the private sector, civil society, development partners and other stakeholders.

Competencies

Core Competencies

- Achieve Results: LEVEL 1: Plans and monitors own work, pay attention to details, deliver quality work by the deadline;

- Think Innovatively: LEVEL 1: Open to creative ideas/known risks, is pragmatic problem solver, makes improvements;

- Learn Continuously: LEVEL 1: Open minded and curious, shares knowledge, learns from mistakes, asks for feedback;

- Adapt with Agility: LEVEL 1: Adapts to change, constructively handles ambiguity/uncertainty, is flexible;

- Act with Determination: LEVEL 1: Shows drive and motivation, able to deliver calmly in face of adversity, confident;

- Engage and Partner: LEVEL 1: Demonstrates compassion/understanding towards others, forms positive relationships;

- Enable Diversity and Inclusion: LEVEL 1: Appreciate/respect differences, aware of unconscious bias, confront discrimination.

Cross-Functional/Technical competencies

Business Management

- Project Management Ability to plan, organize, prioritize and coordinate resources, procedures and stakeholders to achieve project goals and deliver quality outputs on time.

- Partnerships Management Ability to build and maintain effective working relationships with government, United Nations, private-sector, financial-sector, civil society and development partners.

- Monitoring & Evaluation Ability to collect, consolidate and analyze implementation and results information and provide timely, high-quality reporting on progress.

- Communication Ability to draft and edit clear, compelling written and visual content and tailor advocacy messages, formats and channels to diverse audiences.

- Digital Awareness and Literacy Ability to use digital collaboration, content-development, analytics and information-management tools effectively to support program delivery and engagement.

Business Development

- Operations Management Ability to coordinate administrative workflows, procurement, vendors, payments, high-level events, meetings and logistics in accordance with organizational procedures.

Administration and Operations

- Documents and Records Management Ability to organize, retrieve and archive program, event, procurement, vendor, payment and communication records systematically.

Required Skills and Experience

Education

- Secondary education is required or:

- A university degree (Bachelor's degree) in Communications, Journalism, Public Relations, Marketing, Media Studies, Development Studies, Business Administration, Public Administration, Project Management, International Development, Finance, Economics, Social Sciences or a related field will be given due consideration but is not a requirement.

Experience

- A minimum of seven (7) years (with secondary education) or four (4) years (with a Bachelor's degree) of relevant experience in program or project coordination, program administration and operations, stakeholder or private-sector engagement, monitoring and reporting, strategic communications and advocacy, event management, knowledge management or related development-program support functions.

Required skills

- Experience in supporting program or project planning, implementation, coordination and administration;

- Experience in coordinating high-level or technical meetings, forums, workshops or events;

- Experience in maintaining workplans, trackers, minutes and follow-up systems;

- Experience in preparing high-quality briefs, speeches, talking points, articles, reports or presentations;

- Experience in supporting stakeholder engagement, communications, advocacy, procurement, vendors or logistics;

- Experience in using Microsoft Office and digital collaboration tools;

- Strong organizational, drafting, editing experience is required;

- Time-management skills/experience are required.

Desired Skills

- Experience supporting multi-stakeholder programs and working with government, regulators, the United Nations, development partners, financial institutions or the private sector is desirable;

- Experience in sustainable finance, investment promotion or facilitation, innovation, inclusive finance, insurance and risk finance, or related portfolio initiatives is an asset;

- Experience in media relations, digital campaigns, visual storytelling, project or corporate reporting, enterprise resource-planning systems, e-requisitions or non-PO payments is an advantage.

Languages

- Fluency in written and spoken English and Swahili is required.

Professional Certificate

- Professional certification in project management, strategic communications, digital marketing, event management, monitoring and evaluation, procurement, administration or related field is an asset but is not required.

Equal opportunity

As an equal opportunity employer, UNDP values diversity as an expression of the multiplicity of nations and cultures where we operate and, as such, we encourage qualified applicants from all backgrounds to apply for roles in the organization. Our employment decisions are based on merit and suitability for the role, without discrimination.

UNDP is also committed to creating an inclusive workplace where all personnel are empowered to contribute to our mission, are valued, can thrive, and benefit from career opportunities that are open to all.

Sexual harassment, exploitation, and abuse of authority

UNDP does not tolerate harassment, sexual harassment, exploitation, discrimination and abuse of authority. All selected candidates, therefore, undergo relevant checks and are expected to adhere to the respective standards and principles.

Right to select multiple candidates

UNDP reserves the right to select one or more candidates from this vacancy announcement. We may also retain applications and consider candidates applying to this post for other similar positions with UNDP at the same grade level and with similar job description, experience and educational requirements.

Use of AI by candidates

